docker
# Build output, which the image produces for itself. Copying the host's in is
# not just waste: COPY merges into a directory rather than replacing it, so a
# file from an older local build survives the fresh one copied over it - and
# server/front is served to the public, stale source maps included
client/build
server/front
# Developer-local env files, which must stay out of an image layer. The deployed
# build gets its Sentry token as a build secret and the server its configuration
# from the environment, so none of these are needed: a local `docker:build-*`
# would otherwise bake in a personal auth token, or a PORT_OFFSET that points the
# bundle at a port nothing in the container listens on. `.env.local` needs its own
# entry - `.env.*.local` cannot match it, being longer than the name itself
client/.env.sentry-build-plugin
client/.env.local
client/.env.*.local
server/.env.local
server/.env.*.local
server/.env.development
